* [`ag.bad_prime`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Algebraic-geometry/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_ag___bad_prime_FLQQ_--statement) — Prime of bad reduction. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* finiteness of the primes of bad reduction of an abelian variety over a number field, and their identification with the primes dividing its conductor (ag.conductor, partial). Declarations (Tau Ceti): `TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.AbelianVariety`. Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): unproved claim. AbelianVariety.badPrimes (height-one primes at which A has no smooth proper model, the knowl's condition read in Néron-model form, as the docstring explains) formalises the definition, but the knowl's two facts, that there are finitely many bad primes and that they are the primes dividing the conductor, are not formalised. Tau Ceti's finite\_badPrimes is about the bad primes of a Weierstrass equation (used for weak Mordell-Weil), not about abelian varieties of dimension g. Not formalised: “The number of primes of bad reduction is always finite; these are precisely the primes that divide the conductor of \$A\$.”.
* [`ag.bielliptic_curve`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Algebraic-geometry/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_ag___bielliptic_curve_FLQQ_--statement) — Bielliptic curve. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* a precise reading of the three conditions (disjunction or equivalent characterisations, over k or k̄), the Riemann-Hurwitz count of fixed points of an involution, quotients X/⟨w⟩ as curves, and Aut(X) compared with Aut(K(X)/K). Declarations (Tau Ceti): `TauCeti.IsEllipticFunctionField`. Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): reading. As written the three conditions are not equivalent: the first only asks for a 2-to-1 map over k̄, the other two for an involution defined over k (the second also for a k-point of the quotient); a genus-2 curve over ℚ whose two bielliptic involutions are conjugate over ℚ(√2), such as y² = f(x) with x⁶f(2/x) = 8f(x) and f generic (involutions (x, y) ↦ (2/x, ±2√2·y/x³)), satisfies the first and not the others. So 'if and only if it satisfies one of the following conditions' is either a disjunction or a list of characterisations; LeanBridge takes the first as the definition and the involution in function-field form (Aut(X) as Aut(K(X)/K)), and proves no implication between the conditions. Not formalised: “is a \*\*bielliptic curve\*\* if and only if it satisfies one of the following conditions: ... There exists an involution \$w \\in \\Aut(X)\$ defined over \$k\$ such that \$X/\\langle w \\rangle\$ has genus \$1\$ and \$X/\\langle w \\rangle (k) \\ne \\empty\$. - There exists an involution \$w \\in \\Aut(X)\$ defined over \$k\$ having exactly \$2g -2\$ fixed points.”.
