* [`gg.arithmetically_equivalent`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Galois-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_gg___arithmetically_equivalent_FLQQ_--statement) — Arithmetically equivalent siblings. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* Perlis's theorem (Dedekind zeta functions as Artin L-functions of permutation representations, and Chebotarev density). GitHub issues: [#62](https://github.com/CBirkbeck/LeanBridge/issues/62). Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): unproved claim. The formalised count is group-theoretic: the conjugacy classes of subgroups Gassmann equivalent to the point stabiliser, other than its own, independent of the point. That these classes are the arithmetically equivalent fields, which is what makes 'the number of these' a function of G and gives the characterisation \|H1 ∩ C\| = \|H2 ∩ C\|, is Perlis's theorem, proved in no tree (Mathlib has dedekindZeta but nothing comparing the zeta functions of two fields). Not formalised: ““The number of these is a function of \$G\$.” and “By the Galois correspondence, an arithmetically equivalent field corresponds to a conjugacy class of subgroups with representative \$H\_2\$ such that \$\$ \|H\_1 \\cap C\| = \|H\_2\\cap C\|\$\$ for every \{\{KNOWL('gg.conjugacy\_classes', 'conjugacy class')\}\} \$C\$ of \$G\$.””.
