* [`lattice.additively_indecomposable`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___additively_indecomposable_FLQQ_--statement) — Additively indecomposable lattice. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the unimodular converse (the spectral theorem is in Mathlib; its transfer to the lattice and the integrality argument are missing), the A₂ counterexample to the converse (short), and the Mordell–Ko classification in rank ≤ 8 Note: Done: Round AO (AO2\_lattices), 2026-11-23; reviewed (review/AO2\_lattices.md): partial, confirmed; no new Lean (the optional claim of the round was not attempted). Proved (round S2): IsAdditivelyIndecomposable (positive definite, nonzero, the form not a sum of two nonzero symmetric positive semidefinite integral forms) and IsAdditivelyIndecomposable.isOrthogonallyIndecomposable. Nothing in the three trees or Tau Ceti's main (checkout 62bbc05) proves the rest; the tracker's blocker is not stale. Route for the unimodular converse, checked: if B = B\_1 + B\_2 on a unimodular L, B\_i integral, positive semidefinite and nonzero, then f = B\^-1 B\_1 is an integral endomorphism of L (unimodularity), self-adjoint for B, with 0 \<= B(fx, x) \<= B(x, x); its eigenvalues are real and in \[0, 1\] and its characteristic polynomial lies in Z\[t\], so a monic factor without the roots 0 and 1 would have a nonzero integer constant term of absolute value \< 1; hence f\^2 = f and L = f(L) + (1 - f)(L) is an orthogonal splitting with both summands nonzero. The converse fails for A\_2: (2,1;1,2) = (1,1;1,1) + (1,0;0,1), and A\_2 is orthogonally indecomposable since a splitting \<a\> + \<b\> would have ab = 3 with a, b \>= 2. Blocked by: (1) the unimodular converse (route above; Mathlib has the spectral theorem for symmetric operators, the transfer from the lattice and the integrality argument are missing); (2) the A\_2 counterexample to the converse (short, not done); (3) the Mordell-Ko classification of the additively indecomposable lattices of rank \<= 8 (Z, E\_6, E\_7, E\_8), out of scope. Difficulty: hard.
