* [`st_group.ambient`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___ambient_FLQQ_--statement) — Ambient group of a Sato-Tate group. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* Sato–Tate groups of motives (st\_group.definition), and the LMFDB text and code: the dichotomy fails for μ(n) and for SU(2) × μ(n), N(U(1)) × μ(n) with n ≥ 3 (recorded). Note: Done: Round AF (AF5), kept partial, no new Lean. The reading of O(d) as the compact real orthogonal group is a fixable text (recorded) and is formalised (ambientGroup, mem\_ambientGroup\_iff\_of\_even), as is USp(d) (satoTateForm, reindex\_J\_eq\_satoTateForm). Not formalised: the claim that a Sato–Tate group of weight w and degree d lies in USp(d) (w odd) or O(d) (w even). For Sato–Tate groups of motives (st\_group.definition, partial: ℓ-adic realisations, Zariski closure, maximal compact subgroup) it is a theorem about motives, which LeanBridge builds into the bundled SatoTateGroup (field le\_ambientGroup), a model. In the setting the LMFDB uses, its database of groups, it is false for weight 0: LMFDB/lmfdb lmfdb/sato\_tate\_groups/main.py gives the groups μ(n) (labels 0.1.n, mu\_data) weight 0 and degree 1, and st\_ambient returns O(d) for every even weight, so the page of μ(n), n ≥ 3, says 'Contained in O(1)' although μ(3) ⊄ O(1) = \{±1\} (the code's comment: 'only list supgroups with the same ambient (i.e. if mu(n) lies in O(1) don't list supgroups that are not)'). This is the recorded editors' item for st\_group.definition and st\_group.ambient; a fixed text must treat weight 0 apart (U(d), or self-dual motives only). Blocked by st\_group.definition (very hard: motives). Review (AF5): confirmed partial, and the error is wider than weight 0. It is a knowl text error that changes the mathematics, which the display repeats (st\_ambient implements the knowl's parity rule; the data, weight and degree, are right). main.py also generates, for every weight w ≥ 1, the degree-2 groups SU(2) × μ(n) and N(U(1)) × μ(n) (labels w.2.3.cn and w.2.1.dn, su2\_mu\_data and nu1\_mu\_data, 'of any wt \> 0'), the Sato–Tate groups of newforms with a character of order n; for n ≥ 3 they contain the scalar ζI, ζ a primitive n-th root of unity, which lies in no conjugate of USp(2) (det ζI = ζ² ≠ 1) or of O(2) (its eigenvalues ζ, ζ are not closed under complex conjugation), although st\_ambient gives them USp(2) (w odd) or O(2) (w even) as ambient. The dichotomy holds for motives with rational coefficients (a (−1)\^w-symmetric polarization), such as abelian varieties; μ(n) and these groups come from motives with coefficients in Q(ζ\_n) or a Hecke field. Proposed text: '\$d\$ is a subgroup of either:' → '\$d\$ is a subgroup of the unitary group \$\\mathrm\{U\}(d)\$. When it is the Sato-Tate group of a motive with rational coefficients, such as an abelian variety, it is a subgroup of either:', with a third case for the ambient group (U(d)) and the matching change to st\_ambient left to the editors. Blocked by: st\_group.definition (Sato–Tate groups of motives), and the LMFDB's text.
* [`st_group.definition`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___definition_FLQQ_--statement) — Sato-Tate group. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* ℓ-adic Galois representations of motives and abelian varieties, Zariski closure and maximal compact subgroup GitHub issues: [#28](https://github.com/CBirkbeck/LeanBridge/issues/28). Note: Done: SatoTateGroup (LeanBridge: a closed subgroup of U(d) inside USp(d) or O(d), with weight and degree, as the LMFDB stores it) Missing: The Sato–Tate group of a motive or abelian variety: its ℓ-adic Galois representation, the Zariski closure of the image and a maximal compact subgroup, with the Artin-motive case as the image of the Artin representation.
* [`st_group.degree`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___degree_FLQQ_--statement) — Degree of a Sato-Tate group. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* Sato–Tate groups of abelian varieties over number fields with d = 2g (st\_group.definition), the rank 2g of the Tate module (degree 2g of the Frobenius characteristic polynomials of the reductions), and Jacobians of dimension the genus (ag.jacobian). Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): unproved claim. The degree is the matrix size and natDegree\_charpoly proves it is the degree of every characteristic polynomial, but SatoTateGroup records no abelian variety, so d = 2 dim A is not proved. That the Frobenius characteristic polynomials of the reductions have degree 2g, and (a remark the sweep did not quote) that dim Jac(C) is the genus, are both stated as not formalised in LeanBridge's own files. Not formalised: “the degree \$d\$ of its Sato-Tate group is twice its dimension \$g\$ as an abelian variety … if \$A=\\mathrm\{Jac\}(C)\$ is the \{\{KNOWL('ag.jacobian', 'Jacobian')\}\} of a curve \$C\$, then \$g\$ is also the genus of \$C\$ … The degree \$d=2g\$ is then also the degree of the characteristic polynomials of the Frobenius endomorphism of the reductions of \$A\$ modulo good primes.”.
