* [`st_group.hodge_circle`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___hodge_circle_FLQQ_--statement) — Hodge circle. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the LMFDB text (conjugates by G or by G⁰; the condition p + q = w) and Sato–Tate groups of motives with their Hodge numbers (st\_group.definition). Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): reading. IsHodgeCircle takes the G⁰-conjugates of h(U(1)), while the knowl does not say by which group, and the readings differ: for G⁰ = U(1) × U(1) ⊆ USp(4) with a component swapping the factors and h(u) = diag(u, 1, ū, 1), the G-conjugates generate a dense subgroup of G⁰ but the G⁰-conjugates only U(1) × 1. The identification of the h\^\{p,q\} with Hodge numbers of a motive and h\^\{1,0\} = h\^\{0,1\} for abelian varieties are not formalised, and without p + q = w the h\^\{p,q\} are not Hodge numbers (LMFDB issue). Not formalised: “the conjugates of the image of \$h\$ generate a dense subgroup of \$G\^0\$ … When \$G\$ is the Sato-Tate group of a motive \$X\$ of weight \$w\$, the \$h\^\{p,q\}\$ are the Hodge numbers of \$X\$. … For a \$d\$-dimensional abelian variety \$X\$ we necessarily have \$h\^\{0,1\}=h\^\{1,0\}=d\$.”.
* [`st_group.label`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___label_FLQQ_--statement) — Sato-Tate group labels. Difficulty: database, database. *Blocked by:* GAP SmallGroup id of the component group and orderings by moment simplex (letters A, s) left implicit Note: Done: Formalised: labels w.d.A.c.ns as data with their string form, and consistency: w, d, c are the weight, degree and number of components (order of G/G\^0), d and c positive (c \> 0 records that the component group is finite, true for these compact Lie groups but not proved), A an upper-case letter present iff w \> 0, \[c, n\] consistent with the component group as a small group id, s a lower-case letter, n and s omitted only when w = 0 and d = 1. Left out: what A, n and s identify (the identity component, the isomorphism class of the component group, the group) and their values (orderings by moment simplex that the knowl leaves implicit, the SmallGroups numbering); the knowl's claim that G\^0 is trivial in weight 0 holds for motives, not for every SatoTateGroup.
