* [`st_group.subsupgroups`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___subsupgroups_FLQQ_--statement) — Sato-Tate subgroups and supergroups. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* Its five statements: inclusion up to conjugacy is a partial order on the groups with a given G⁰; finitely many rational ones, with no unique maximal one in general; finitely many maximal subgroups (easy from G⁰ of finite index); infinitely many minimal supergroups in general (e.g. the trivial group in O(2)); finitely many minimal rational supergroups. Rationality rests on st\_group.rational's gap. Note: Done: The notions are LeanBridge's: maximal subgroups ('SatoTateGroup.IsMaximalSubgroup'), minimal supergroups ('SatoTateGroup.IsMinimalSupergroup', 'SatoTateGroup.IsMinimalRationalSupergroup'), rationality ('SatoTateGroup.IsRational') and G⁰ of finite index ('SatoTateGroup.finiteIndex\_connectedComponentOfOne').
* [`st_group.supgroups`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___supgroups_FLQQ_--statement) — Minimal supergroups. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the knowl's text ("maximal if it has no supergroups" read as "no rational supergroups", a question for the editors) and st\_group.rational's gap: IsRational assumes, unproved, that Tau Ceti's rational Weyl modules give all irreducible characters of GL\_d(ℂ). Note: Done: The gap is closed: H\^0 = G\^0 is proved for every proper finite-index supergroup H of a Sato-Tate group G (closed, in the ambient group), so for the minimal and the minimal rational supergroups, through the general Subgroup.map\_connectedComponentOfOne\_eq\_of\_le (a closed finite-index subgroup K of L is open in L, so L\^0 lies in K and is the identity component of K). Still partial, by the ruling of 11-04 on readings: 'A rational Sato-Tate group is maximal if it has no supergroups' is formalised (SatoTateGroup.IsMaximal) as 'has no rational proper finite-index supergroup', and the readings differ: in weight 0 the dihedral group D\_6 of order 12 in O(2) is rational (its character values are integers, 2cos(pi/3) = 1) and its finite-index supergroups D\_12, D\_18, ... are all irrational (trace 2cos(pi/3k) of a generating rotation of D\_6k, k \>= 2), so it is maximal on the formalised reading but not on the literal one. The knowl's previous sentence ('we list only rational minimal supergroups') and the LMFDB code (a stored 'maximal' column, set in code only for mu(n), SU(2) x mu(n), N(U(1)) x mu(n), where both readings agree) do not decide it. Review: a second gap, not found by the author, keeps the knowl partial even if the reading is accepted: IsMinimalRationalSupergroup and IsMaximal rest on IsRational/IsRationalSubgroup, whose characters are those of Tau Ceti's rational Weyl modules, taken as all the irreducible characters of GL\_d(C) although that they exhaust them is unproved; st\_group.rational is partial for exactly this (sweep of 11-06), and its two rational notions inherit it. Blocked by: the reading of 'maximal' (question recorded for the editors) and the highest-weight classification behind st\_group.rational.
* [`st_group.trace_moments`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___trace_moments_FLQQ_--statement) — Trace moments of a Sato-Tate group. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the symmetry of the distributions of t and a₁, proved only when −1 ∈ G: it fails for the committed SatoTateGroup (the trivial subgroup of USp(2) has E\[t\] = 2), whose structure has no Hodge or motivic axiom; it holds for Sato-Tate groups of motives. Note: Done: Integrality is now proved: traceMoment\_eq\_finrank\_invariants gives E\[t\^n\] = dim of the G-invariants in the n-th tensor power of C\^d (tensorPowerRep, the restriction of LeanBridge's ambientTensorRep, whose character is t\^n by trace\_tensorRep), through Tau Ceti's integral\_character\_eq\_finrank\_invariants: a nonnegative integer. The distribution of t is now explicit (traceDistribution, the pushforward of Haar measure under the trace) and M\_n\[t\] is its n-th moment; t = -a\_1 holds in every weight and degree (charpolyCoeff\_one\_eq\_neg\_trace, degree 0 included). Still partial: 'the moment sequences of t and a\_1 coincide because their distributions are symmetric about zero' in odd weight is proved only when -1 lies in G (traceDistribution\_map\_neg, moment\_charpolyCoeff\_one\_eq\_traceMoment; odd moments vanish by traceMoment\_eq\_zero\_of\_odd). It is false for LeanBridge's SatoTateGroup, which has no Hodge circle or rationality axiom: the trivial subgroup of USp(2) in weight 1 has E\[t\] = 2 and E\[a\_1\] = -2. It holds for Sato-Tate groups of motives (-1 is the image of -1 under a Hodge circle with p + q = w odd), and the committed IsHodgeCircle does not even force p + q = w, so it cannot supply -1 either.
* [`st_group.trace_zero_density`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Sato-Tate-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_st_group___trace_zero_density_FLQQ_--statement) — Trace zero density of a Sato-Tate group. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the Sato–Tate axioms on LeanBridge's SatoTateGroup (a Hodge circle in G⁰ whose Hodge numbers vanish off p + q = w, the gap of st\_group.hodge\_circle and st\_group.definition): the odd-weight clause holds only under that hypothesis Note: Done: Round AO (AO3\_sato\_tate\_lie), 2026-11-23; the author proposed formalised, the review (review/AO3\_sato\_tate\_lie.md) keeps it partial, hard. Blocked by: the Sato-Tate axioms on LeanBridge's SatoTateGroup (a Hodge circle in G\^0 whose Hodge numbers h\^\{p,q\} vanish off p + q = w, the gap of st\_group.hodge\_circle and st\_group.definition): the true part of the wrong clause, 'constant and then equal to zero when the weight is odd', is proved only under that hypothesis (eq\_zero\_of\_trace\_eq\_of\_hasHodgeEigenvalues) and is false for SatoTateGroup without it (the trivial subgroup of USp(2), weight 1, has the constant trace 2), exactly as for st\_group.trace\_moments; dropping the clause instead (the shorter correction recorded on 11-07) drops a claim true for every Sato-Tate group of a motive of odd weight (the GL\_2 precedent). Question put to the user in the review. Proved, for every SatoTateGroup with its normalised Haar measure: the trace zero density (SatoTateGroup.traceZeroDensity, committed); 'on each component the trace is either constant \[...\] or varies continuously taking each particular value with probability zero' (trace\_const\_or\_forall\_measure\_eq\_zero, from IsEntryPolynomial.forall\_eq\_or\_measure\_eq\_zero, Serre's Proposition 8.4.3.3 proved as for st\_group.probabilities; the trace is continuous, IsEntryPolynomial.continuous with isEntryPolynomial\_trace); 'so this probability is simply the ratio of the number of components on which the trace is identically zero to the total number of components' (traceZeroDensity\_eq\_card\_div\_card; it gives the LMFDB's displayed values 0 for mu(n), SU(2) and U(1), 1/2 for N(U(1))). The stale half of the old blocker: the finiteness of the components (Components.lean). Text error, recorded on 11-07 (lmfdb-knowl-edits-2026-11-07.md, the shorter correction): 'constant and equal to zero' is false for the LMFDB's weight-0 groups mu(n), e.g. 0.1.1 with trace 1. The author's correction, which keeps the true part: old 'On each component of a Sato-Tate group, the trace is either constant and equal to zero, or varies continuously taking each particular value with probability zero;', new 'On each component of a Sato-Tate group, the trace is either constant (and then equal to zero when the weight is odd), or varies continuously taking each particular value with probability zero;' (it should replace the 11-07 one). Proved towards it: a constant trace is zero when -1 lies in the identity component (eq\_zero\_of\_trace\_eq\_of\_neg\_one\_mem, for every SatoTateGroup); a Hodge circle h with Hodge numbers vanishing off p + q = w, w odd, has h(-1) = -1 (matrix\_eq\_neg\_one\_of\_hasHodgeEigenvalues, the inner step of the committed neg\_one\_mem\_of\_hasHodgeEigenvalues), so then a constant trace is zero (eq\_zero\_of\_trace\_eq\_of\_hasHodgeEigenvalues); the hypotheses are met by U(1) (torusU1HodgeCircle, torusU1HodgeCircle\_mem, hasHodgeEigenvalues\_torusU1HodgeCircle: h\^\{1,0\} = h\^\{0,1\} = 1). Checks (review): as for st\_group.probabilities.
