# L-functions

* [`intro.tutorial`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/L-functions/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_intro___tutorial_FLQQ_--statement) — A tour of the Riemann zeta function. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* The verification that the first 10¹³ zeros lie on the critical line (very hard), the first zero at 1/2 + 14.1347…i (rigorous numerics, hard) and Riemann's link between primes and zeros; RH and GRH are open conjectures. Declarations (Mathlib): `zeta_eq_tsum_one_div_nat_cpow`, `riemannZeta_one_sub`, `riemannZeta_eulerProduct_tprod`, `RiemannHypothesis`. Note: Done: The Dirichlet series, functional equation and Euler product of ζ are in Mathlib ('zeta\_eq\_tsum\_one\_div\_nat\_cpow', 'riemannZeta\_one\_sub', 'riemannZeta\_eulerProduct\_tprod'), with RH as a statement ('RiemannHypothesis'). LeanBridge has ζ as an analytic L-function with Selberg data (1, 1, (0:), 1) ('AnalyticLFunction.zeta'), the Z-function ('AnalyticLFunction.zFunction') and GRH as a statement ('GeneralizedRiemannHypothesis').
* [`lfunction.central_character`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/L-functions/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lfunction___central_character_FLQQ_--statement) — Central character of an L-function. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* The existence, for every analytic L-function of degree d and conductor N, of a Dirichlet character χ mod N with top Euler coefficients (−1)\^d χ(p): not implied by the class's axioms, and open. Note: Done: Round AF (AF2). The gap is unchanged by item 2 of the brief: the knowl asserts that every L-function has Euler factors 1 + a\_p x + ... + (-1)\^d chi(p) x\^d for a Dirichlet character chi mod N, so with lfunction read as AnalyticLFunction this must hold for every AnalyticLFunction, and it is not among the axioms and not known to follow from them (they only fix the degrees of the Euler factors, d exactly for p not dividing N, with nothing tying the top coefficients at different primes). The predicate, its uniqueness by Dirichlet's theorem and the vanishing at p \| N are proved (CentralCharacter.lean). New: for the analytic L-function of a newform (built in this round, see lfunction.conductor) the nebentypus is the central character, isCentralCharacter\_analyticLFunction: the T\^2 coefficient of the Euler factor 1 - a\_p T + chi(p) T\^2 is (-1)\^2 chi(p). AF2 review: confirmed partial; the existence for every analytic L-function is not a consequence of the axioms and is not known (it follows from the expected automorphy of every L-function), so it stays partial as a conjecture would. Side finding for the editors: 'L\_p(x) = 1 + a\_p x + ...' has the opposite sign to the Dirichlet coefficient a\_p (cmf.lfunction writes 1 - a\_p p\^\{-s\}; for d = 1 the term a\_p x is -chi(p) x); it does not affect the central character. Blocked by: the existence, for every analytic L-function of degree d and conductor N, of a Dirichlet character chi mod N with top Euler coefficients (-1)\^d chi(p) (not implied by the class's axioms; open). Not formalised: “An L-function has an Euler product of the form \$L(s) = \\prod\_p L\_p(p\^\{-s\})\^\{-1\}\$ where \$L\_p(x) = 1 + a\_p x + \\ldots + (-1)\^d \\chi(p) x\^d\$. The character \$\\chi\$ is a Dirichlet character mod \$N\$”.
* [`lfunction.coefficient_field`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/L-functions/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lfunction___coefficient_field_FLQQ_--statement) — Coefficient field of an L-function. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* finiteness over Q of the field generated by the arithmetically normalised Dirichlet coefficients of an arithmetic L-function (and their integrality), not implied by the AnalyticLFunction axioms Declarations (Mathlib): `IntermediateField.adjoin`. Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): unproved claim. The field is the unnamed composite IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ \{L.arithmeticCoeff L.motivicWeight n\}. Nothing proves that it is finite over ℚ, which does not follow from 'arithmetic' (lfunction.arithmetic only asks the a\_n to be algebraic). The a\_n n\^\{w/2\} are algebraic integers only under the hypothesis that some such w exists: otherwise motivicWeight takes the junk value 0. Not formalised: “is the number field generated by the algebraic integers \$a\_nn\^\{w/2\}\$”.
* [`lfunction.conductor`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/L-functions/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lfunction___conductor_FLQQ_--statement) — Conductor of an L-function. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* The L-function of a Maass cusp newform of level N as an AnalyticLFunction of conductor N (the user's ruling of 2026-11-12: 'cusp form on GL(2)/ℚ' covers Maass forms). Declarations (Mathlib): `DirichletCharacter.IsPrimitive`. Note: Done: Round AF (AF2). The three recorded gaps are closed. (1) Uniqueness of the Selberg data: no longer required (ruling of 2026-11-12); the conductor is the N of the bundled functional equation, the second component of the Selberg data (AnalyticLFunction.conductor, SelbergData.conductor). (2) Primitive Dirichlet characters: round AB's AnalyticLFunction.dirichlet hchi has conductor N (conductor\_dirichlet), and hchi : chi.IsPrimitive is by definition chi.conductor = N. (3) Newforms, the shared gap with cmf.analytic\_rank and cmf.fricke: HeckeRing.GL2.Newform.analyticLFunction hk is the L-function of a newform f of level N and weight k \>= 1 (Tau Ceti's Newform N k) as an AnalyticLFunction: coefficients a\_n = A\_n / n\^\{(k-1)/2\}, Euler factors 1 - a\_p T + chi(p) T\^2 (degree 2 exactly for p not dividing N, from Tau Ceti's Hecke recursion and multiplicativity), gamma factor Gamma\_C(s + (k-1)/2) (Selberg data SelbergData.newform, written Gamma\_R(s) Gamma\_R(s + 1) for k = 1 since the LMFDB asks re nu \> 0), continuation, completion and functional equation Lambda(s) = epsilon conj Lambda(1 - conj s) with epsilon = i\^k lambda\_N(f) from the committed LFunctionSign.lean (built on Tau Ceti's Hecke functional equation), and conductor N (conductor\_analyticLFunction). The new analytic input is absolute convergence for re s \> 1, beyond Hecke's 3/2: Mathlib's mean-square bound sum\_\{n\<M\} \|A\_n\|\^2 = O(M\^k) with Cauchy-Schwarz gives sum\_\{n\<=M\} \|A\_n\| = O(M\^\{(k+1)/2\}), so the coefficient series of any cusp form converges