* [`lfunction.trivial_zero`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/L-functions/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lfunction___trivial_zero_FLQQ_--statement) — Trivial zeros of an L-function. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* The last sentence, corrected to 'certain integers' in the arithmetic normalization (recorded), for every arithmetic L-function: open in general (it excludes Maass forms with algebraic eigenvalues); true for motivic L-functions, which have no notion in the trees. Note: Done: Round AF (AF2), on item 2 of the brief (lfunction is AnalyticLFunction). New in TrivialZeroOrder.lean, for every AnalyticLFunction: the poles of the gamma factor L\_inf are exactly the points of gammaPoles (meromorphicOrderAt\_gammaFactor\_neg\_iff, via the entire function 1 / L\_inf), so trivialZeros is literally 'the zeros at poles of the gamma factors'; for every s, ord\_s L = ord\_s Lambda + ord\_s (1 / L\_inf) (meromorphicOrderAt\_toFun), from Lambda = N\^\{s/2\} L\_inf L off the poles and s = 0, 1 (AD's completed\_eq\_of\_notMem\_gammaPoles) and the meromorphy of Lambda everywhere; hence a pole of L\_inf at which Lambda has no pole is a trivial zero, of order at least that of the pole (mem\_trivialZeros\_of\_mem\_gammaPoles), and since Lambda is holomorphic off \{0, 1\} and 1 is never a pole, every pole s != 0 of L\_inf is a trivial zero (gammaPoles\_diff\_zero\_subset\_trivialZeros). The exception at s = 0 ('except possibly from Riemann zeta-function factors') is genuine: Gamma\_R(s) has a pole at 0 and zeta(0) = -1/2 (zero\_notMem\_trivialZeros\_zeta), which also shows the hypothesis on Lambda is not vacuous to drop. No zeros or poles for re s \> 1: toFun\_ne\_zero (CriticalStrip) and the axiom differentiableOn\_toFun. Remaining gap, the last sentence. As written it is wrong: in the analytic normalisation the poles of a weight-k newform are at -(k-1)/2 - n, not integers, and in the arithmetic normalisation 0 is a pole of Gamma\_R(s) (zeta, even Dirichlet characters, with L(0, chi) = 0 a trivial zero for even chi != 1) and of Gamma\_C(s) (holomorphic newforms, L(f, 0) = 0). Corrected (AF2 review): 'For arithmetic L-functions, in the arithmetic normalization, the poles are at certain integers.' The author's 'non-positive integers' is false too: from motivic weight 2 on, gamma factors Gamma\_C(s - p) with p \>= 1 occur in the arithmetic normalization; for example L(Sym\^3 E, s) of a non-CM elliptic curve E over Q (motivic weight 3, entire completion by Kim-Shahidi) has the gamma factor Gamma\_C(s) Gamma\_C(s - 1), analytically Gamma\_C(s + 3/2) Gamma\_C(s + 1/2), so L(Sym\^3 E, 1) = 0 is a trivial zero at a positive integer. Even corrected it is a claim about every arithmetic L-function (algebraic Dirichlet coefficients) that is not known: a Maass cusp form of eigenvalue 1/4 + r\^2, r != 0, with algebraic Hecke eigenvalues would give an AnalyticLFunction with algebraic coefficients and poles at -2n -+ ir, and that none exists is open; it holds for motivic L-functions, of which LeanBridge has no general notion. Also recorded (AF2 review): 'the L-function must have zeros at poles of the gamma factors' fails for zeta at s = 0; the corrected 'at the poles of the gamma factors at which the completed L-function has no pole' is what mem\_trivialZeros\_of\_mem\_gammaPoles and gammaPoles\_diff\_zero\_subset\_trivialZeros prove (fixable text, ruling of 2026-11-11). '(except possibly from Riemann zeta-function factors)' is read as the class's axiom that Lambda is holomorphic off \{0, 1\}, as for lfunction.completed. Blocked by: the last sentence, corrected to integers in the arithmetic normalization, for every arithmetic L-function (open in general: it excludes algebraic Maass eigenvalues; true for motivic L-functions, which have no notion in the trees). Not formalised: “For arithmetic L-functions, the poles are at certain negative integers.”
