* [`mf.bianchi.bianchigroup`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Bianchi-modular-forms/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_mf___bianchi___bianchigroup_FLQQ_--statement) — Bianchi Groups. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* (a) the finite volume of G \\ ℋ₃ when 𝒪\_K is not a principal ideal domain (class number \> 1), which needs reduction theory with h cusps; proved when 𝒪\_K is a PID (covolume\_bianchiSubgroup\_lt\_top), and the class number one of d = 19, 43, 67, 163 is on Tau Ceti's main only; (b) 'hyperbolic 3-fold': G \\ ℋ₃ is an orbifold (S fixes j), and no tree has orbifolds. Proved for every imaginary quadratic K: PSL(2, 𝒪\_K) is a discrete subgroup of PSL(2, ℂ) = Isom⁺(ℋ₃), acting properly discontinuously, with a fundamental domain and a non-compact quotient (round AL). Both gaps stand under the standing rulings: finite volume is true for every K, so class number one alone is strictly weaker; '3-fold' read as 'the quotient' would drop the orbifold structure. Declarations (Mathlib): `Matrix.ProjectiveSpecialLinearGroup`, `NumberField.RingOfIntegers`. Note: Done: Round AL (AL2\_picard\_group), optional progress, with the lead's follow-up; reviewed (review/AL2\_picard\_group.md); stays partial. Take K a number field with finrank ℚ K = 2 and IsTotallyComplex K (an imaginary quadratic field), embedded by σ, and PSL(2, 𝒪\_K) Mathlib's ProjectiveSpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) (𝓞 K). New results: (1) PSL(2, 𝒪\_K) is a subgroup of PSL(2, ℂ): bianchiToPSL σ := TauCeti.SpecialLinear.projectiveMap 2 (σ ∘ algebraMap) is injective (bianchiToPSL\_injective, for any field K, through HyperbolicThreeSpace.projectiveMap\_injective), with image bianchiSubgroup σ. Before this round only the images of subgroups of GL₂(𝒪\_K) in PSL₂(ℂ) were treated, never PSL(2, 𝒪\_K) itself. (2) Discrete: discreteTopology\_bianchiSubgroup. (3) Acts properly discontinuously on ℋ₃ (properlyDiscontinuousSMul\_bianchiSubgroup), with a measurable fundamental domain (exists\_isFundamentalDomain\_bianchiSubgroup). (4) The quotient G \\ ℋ₃ is not compact for every imaginary quadratic K (not\_compactSpace\_quotient\_bianchiSubgroup): units of 𝒪\_K have absolute value 1 (norm\_unit\_eq\_one) and nonzero integers at least 1 (one\_le\_norm\_apply). PSL(2, ℂ)/G is not compact either (not\_compactSpace\_quotientGroup\_bianchiSubgroup). (5) Finite volume when 𝒪\_K is a principal ideal domain (covolume\_bianchiSubgroup\_lt\_top; fundamental domains exist, so Mathlib's covolume is not its junk value 0). This uses the committed reduction theory exists\_le\_height\_smul and a translation by 𝒪\_K into a Siegel set of finite volume. The hypothesis is met, for example, by ℚ(i) = QuadraticAlgebra ℚ (-1) 0 (isNatEuclidean\_ringOfIntegers\_quadraticAlgebra with IsNatEuclidean.isPrincipalIdealRing; the review instantiated covolume\_bianchiSubgroup\_lt\_top there, scratch AL2\_picard\_group-review/Probe.lean), and by the five Euclidean fields d = 1, 2, 3, 7, 11 of the LMFDB's newforms (dq.mf.bianchi.source), proved PID in LeanBridge. (6) 'PSL₂(ℂ) can be identified with the group of orientation-preserving isometries of ℋ₃' is now proved (follow-up, OrientationPreservingIsometry.lean), so it is no longer a blocker: pslEquivOrientationPreservingIsom : PSL(2, ℂ) ≃\* Isom⁺(ℋ₃), for Tau Ceti's smooth Riemannian isometries of ds = \|dz\|/y, orientation-preserving meaning a positive Jacobian determinant in the chart ℂ × ℝ = ℝ³ (IsOrientationPreserving, jacobianDet; the sign is the same at every point, isOrientationPreserving\_iff\_jacobianDet\_pos), acting by the LMFDB's formula (pslToIsom\_apply) and by z ↦ (az + b)(cz + d)⁻¹ in the quaternions (toQuaternion\_smul). Blocked by: (a) the finite volume of G \\ ℋ₃ for an imaginary quadratic K whose 𝒪\_K is not a principal ideal domain (class number \> 1), which needs reduction theory with h cusps: hard. In the LMFDB's setting all fields have class number one (d = 1, 2, 3, 7, 11 for newforms, Rahm's dimension data also d = 19, 43, 67, 163); for d = 19, 43, 67, 163 the PID property is on Tau Ceti's main only (TauCeti.classNumber\_eq\_one\_of\_mem\_heegnerNumbers, TauCeti.NumberField.classNumber\_eq\_one\_of\_mem\_heegnerDiscriminants, with Mathlib's NumberField.classNumber\_eq\_one\_iff), so that case waits for the bump. (b) 'hyperbolic 3-fold': G \\ ℋ₃ is a hyperbolic 3-orbifold, not a manifold, since PSL(2, 𝒪\_K) has torsion (S = (0, -1; 1, 0) has order 2 and fixes j); no tree, Tau Ceti's main included, has orbifolds. The review asked which reading 'Let K be an imaginary quadratic field' and 'hyperbolic 3-fold' should get (review/AL2\_picard\_group.md); the lead settled it on the standing rulings and reported it to the user: finite volume is true for every K and proved only for class number one (a claim proved on part of the range where it is true is partial), and reading '3-fold' as 'the quotient' would drop the true orbifold structure (a correction must keep every true claim), so both stay blockers. Difficulty: hard. Verified with the files of mf.maass.picard: lake build, \#lint, \#print axioms standard, dup\_decls 0 clashes.
* [`mf.bianchi.cm`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Bianchi-modular-forms/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_mf___bianchi___cm_FLQQ_--statement) — CM Bianchi newforms. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* that the character of a CM Bianchi eigenform is quadratic (strong multiplicity one) Note: Done: Partial by the user's ruling of 2026-11-04 (whenever it is doubtful whether a knowl is formalised or partial, it is partial): that the character χ is quadratic is not proved, the gap that keeps mf.cm partial. Blocked by: that the character of a CM Bianchi eigenform is quadratic (strong multiplicity one). Earlier record: CM is a condition on the Hecke eigensystem: a nontrivial Hecke character chi : I(m) -\> C\^x of K (LMFDB character.hecke, IsIdealHeckeCharacter, any order) with a\_P = chi(P) a\_P at every prime P not dividing the level and prime to m. It is defined on every Hecke eigenform of IsBianchiHeckeEigenform (weight k, any level, imaginary quadratic K of any class number, a class containing every Bianchi newform), which needs neither the weight-k Whittaker normalisation nor the algebraicity of the eigenvalues that keep the round of 31 October's newforms partial (the condition is also invariant under rescaling the eigenvalues); the same predicate on the round of 31 October's BianchiNewformOfWeight inherits their partial status for k \> 2. Proved a\_P = 0 wherever chi(P) != 1 (in particular -1), that ray class characters are Hecke characters, and that in weight 2 CM by a ray class character is the round of 29 October's notion; as in the knowl, CM is defined by the eigenvalue characterisation (the twist is not constructed), and that chi is necessarily quadratic is not proved.
