* [`cmf.distinguishing_primes`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Classical-modular-forms/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_cmf___distinguishing_primes_FLQQ_--statement) — Distinguishing Hecke operators. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* strong multiplicity one for isobaric sums of the conjugates on traces of Satake parameters (the method of Jacquet–Shalika, resting on L(1, h × conj h') ≠ 0 for h ≠ h', Shahidi 1981), or else the Galois representations of Deligne and Deligne–Serre with Brauer–Nesbitt GitHub issues: [#35](https://github.com/CBirkbeck/LeanBridge/issues/35). Note: Done: The LMFDB sequence is formalised by its greedy rule (each p\_m the least prime p not dividing N with c(\{p\_1..p\_\{m-1\},p\}) \> c(\{p\_1..p\_\{m-1\}\}), for the number c of distinct families of Hecke charpolys over the newforms of S\_k\^new(N, chi), stopping when no prime p not dividing N increases the count), proved to exist and be unique, to be the set of primes p not dividing N at which the count along all such primes jumps, strictly increasing, of length less than the number of Galois orbits when the space is nonzero, empty for a single orbit, and distinguishing exactly when all primes not dividing N do (the round of 31 October and ZQ0, which also record the knowl's errors). This round adds that a newform with a single Galois conjugate is determined by its Hecke charpolys at the primes not dividing N among the newforms of its level, weight and nebentypus, so those primes distinguish the newforms of a space all of whose Galois orbits have a single element. DistinguishesNewforms N k chi \{p \| not p divides N\} in general remains unproved: strong multiplicity one is not enough, since the charpolys record the multisets \{a\_p(h) : h in \[f\]\} one prime at a time. The missing theorem is strong multiplicity one for the isobaric sums of the conjugates, on the traces of their Satake parameters, in the manner of Jacquet-Shalika (On Euler products and the classification of automorphic forms II), resting on L(1, h x conj h') != 0 for h != h' (Shahidi, On certain L-functions), or else the Galois representations of Deligne and Deligne-Serre with the Brauer-Nesbitt theorem (Tau Ceti has the Chebotarev density theorem). None of those is at the pins or on Tau Ceti's main, and the weak Rankin-Selberg theorems proved in this round exclude a pole at sigma = 1 for different nebentypes but do not exclude a zero.
* [`cmf.eisenstein_form`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Classical-modular-forms/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_cmf___eisenstein_form_FLQQ_--statement) — Holomorphic Eisenstein modular form. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* the Petersson orthogonality of the E\_k\^\{χ₁,χ₂\}(dτ) to cusp forms (so that their span is the orthogonal complement E\_k(N,χ)), and the weights 1 and 2 with E\_2\^\{1,1\}(τ) − dE\_2\^\{1,1\}(dτ); Tau Ceti main has the span of the series of nebentypus χ only as some complement of S\_k(N,χ) for k ≥ 3 (isCompl\_eisensteinSubspace\_range\_cuspToModFormCharSpace). Note: Done: Partial by the sweep of 2026-11-06 (the user's rulings: when in doubt, partial; an unproved fact stated in the knowl makes it partial; a model of the knowl's object is partial): unproved claim. LeanBridge defines E\_k(Γ) as the Petersson-orthogonal complement of S\_k(Γ) and proves M = E ⊕ S, but nothing identifies E\_k(N,χ) with the span of the E\_k\^\{χ₁,χ₂\}(dτ); its docstring says the equality with Tau Ceti's spans 'is not proved here'. Tau Ceti main proves only that the span of the series (k ≥ 3) is a complement of S\_k(N,χ), and two complements of S\_k need not coincide without the orthogonality, so main also has the gap, besides weights 1 and 2. Not formalised: “The space \$E\_k(N, \\chi)\$ is spanned by the \$E\_k\^\{\\chi\_1, \\chi\_2\}(d \\tau)\$ where \$\\chi\_1 \\chi\_2 = \\chi\$ and \$d N\_1 N\_2 \\mid N\$, unless \$k = 2\$ and \$\\chi = 1\$, in which case \$E\_2\^\{1,1\}(d \\tau)\$ is not holomorphic, and is replaced by \$E\_2\^\{1,1\}(\\tau) - d E\_2\^\{1,1\}(d \\tau)\$.”.
