* [`sh.level`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_sh___level_FLQQ_--statement) — Level of a Shimura curve. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* shimcurve.eichler (for every prime p at which B ramifies and every Eichler order O of B, p divides discrd(O) exactly once) Note: Done: Round AJ (AJ3): still partial. 'The level of a Shimura curve is the level of the associated Eichler order': the level of an Eichler order is now unique when it exists (IsEichlerOrderOfLevel.unique) and exists exactly when every ramified prime divides discrd(O) exactly once (exists\_isEichlerOrderOfLevel\_iff), so the level is a well-defined function on the Eichler orders that have one; that every Eichler order has one is shimcurve.eichler's open claim (for the curves X(D,M;1) = X\_0(D;M) of shimcurve.xdm1 the order is Eichler of level M by hypothesis, in Standard.lean's StandardShimuraCurve.eichler). The other blockers are stale: discrd(O) and disc(B) are formalised (shimcurve.disco and shimcurve.discb, round ZZ7), and 'Shimura curves themselves' is the modcurve.level case, as for sh.disc: the knowl defines an invariant of the curve through its data (the order O), with no theorem about the curve. Blocked by: shimcurve.eichler (for every prime p at which B ramifies and every Eichler order O of B, p divides discrd(O) exactly once). Difficulty: hard. Review (AJ3): partial confirmed. 'The associated Eichler order' is data of the curve (X(D,M;1) = X\_0(D;M)), not a fact about it; the sentence needs the level of every Eichler order to exist, which is shimcurve.eichler's open claim (whose local-global half is on Tau Ceti main only, so it also waits for the bump).
* [`shimcurve.decomposition`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_shimcurve___decomposition_FLQQ_--statement) — Isogeny decomposition of the Jacobian of a Shimura curve. Difficulty: very hard, theory. *Blocked by:* Shimura curves X\_H and their Jacobians; modular abelian varieties and Jacquet–Langlands for the factor labels Note: Done: TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.AbelianVariety.IsIsogenyDecomposition applied to a Jacobian, TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.AbelianVariety.IsJacobian (LeanBridge) Missing: Shimura curves X\_H and their Jacobians, and the modular abelian varieties of weight-2 newforms with the Jacquet–Langlands correspondence that label the factors.
* [`shimcurve.eichler`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_shimcurve___eichler_FLQQ_--statement) — Eichler Orders. Difficulty: hard, theory. *Blocked by:* for every prime p at which B ramifies and every Eichler order O of B, p divides discrd(O) exactly once; that is, the local theory at a ramified prime: the elements of the division algebra B ⊗ Q\_p with integral reduced norm form its unique maximal Z\_p-order (v\_p ∘ nrd is an ultrametric valuation there), of reduced discriminant p, with the local-global correspondence for orders (O is maximal iff O ⊗ Z\_p is maximal for every p, and discrd(O) is read prime by prime). The local maximal order is in neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti (pin or main); Tau Ceti main, not the pin, has the local-global dictionary for lattices (TauCeti.glueCompletions, TauCeti.eq\_glueCompletions\_iff, RingTheory/DedekindDomain/AdicValuation/Gluing.lean) and the uniqueness of the quaternion division algebra over a local field (TauCeti.QuaternionAlgebra.nonempty\_algEquiv\_of\_hilbertSymbol\_eq\_neg\_one), so a proof through them waits for the bump Note: Done: Round AJ (AJ3): partial progress; the knowl's claim is not proved in general. New file EichlerOrderLevel.lean, for a quaternion algebra B over Q (central simple of dimension 4): (1) the index formula discrd(O) = \[O' : O\] discrd(O') for orders O \<= O' (quaternionDiscrd\_eq\_relIndex\_mul, from disc(O) = \[O' : O\]\^2 disc(O') by Mathlib's AddSubgroup.relIndex\_eq\_abs\_det and Tau Ceti's IsLattice.toAddSubgroup\_eq\_closure\_range\_extendOfIsLattice); (2) every order lies in a maximal order (exists\_isMaximalQuaternionOrder\_le: an order of least reduced discriminant above it), so Eichler orders exist (exists\_isEichlerOrder\_matrix for M\_2(Q)); (3) an Eichler order is an order (IsEichlerOrder.isQuaternionOrder); (4) the level is unique when it exists (IsEichlerOrderOfLevel.unique); (5) the exact reduction of the knowl's claim: an Eichler order O has a level iff every prime at which B ramifies divides discrd(O) exactly once (exists\_isEichlerOrderOfLevel\_iff, as disc(B) is the product of the ramified primes); (6) so the claim holds whenever no prime ramifies (exists\_isEichlerOrderOfLevel\_of\_discriminant\_eq\_one), for instance for M\_2(Q) (QuaternionAlgebra.discriminant\_matrix, exists\_isEichlerOrderOfLevel\_matrix), which also shows that the committed IsEichlerOrderOfLevel is not vacuous. By (1), for O = O\_1 ∩ O\_2 the claim is equivalent to discrd(O\_1) = disc(B) together with \[O\_1 : O\] prime to disc(B); the split primes play no role. Not proved: the claim for B ramified somewhere, the case of the LMFDB's Shimura curves (indefinite division algebras). Overlap with Tau Ceti main (not at the pin): TauCeti.IntegralOrder.IsOrder.exists\_le\_isMaximalOrder (TauCeti/RingTheory/IntegralOrder/Maximal.lean) proves the existence of maximal orders in general; (2) here is a short corollary of (1), to rebase at the bump. Main has nothing on Eichler orders, quaternion orders or their discriminants (searched: Eichler, quaternion order, discrd, reduced discriminant, type number). Text error, a reading the formalisation already uses: the knowl writes the reduced discriminant as disc(O), which shimcurve.disco uses for the discriminant (= discrd(O)\^2), and does not define D. Old: "is of the form \$\\mathrm\{disc\}(O)=D\\cdot M\$ with \$\\gcd(D,M)=1\$"; new: "is of the form \$\\operatorname\{discrd\}(O)=D\\cdot M\$ with \$D=\\operatorname\{disc\}(B)\$ and \$\\gcd(D,M)=1\$". Blocked by: for every prime p at which B ramifies and every Eichler order O of B, p divides discrd(O) exactly once; that is, the local theory at a ramified prime: the elements of the division algebra B ⊗ Q\_p with integral reduced norm form its unique maximal Z\_p-order (v\_p ∘ nrd is an ultrametric valuation there), of reduced discriminant p, with the local-global correspondence for orders (O is maximal iff O ⊗ Z\_p is maximal for every p, and discrd(O) is read prime by prime). The local maximal order is in neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti (pin or main); Tau Ceti main, not the pin, has the local-global dictionary for lattices (TauCeti.glueCompletions, TauCeti.eq\_glueCompletions\_iff, RingTheory/DedekindDomain/AdicValuation/Gluing.lean) and the uniqueness of the quaternion division algebra over a local field (TauCeti.QuaternionAlgebra.nonempty\_algEquiv\_of\_hilbertSymbol\_eq\_neg\_one), so a proof through them waits for the bump. Difficulty: hard. Review (AJ3): partial confirmed. The reduction is exact (exists\_isEichlerOrderOfLevel\_iff, both directions checked), the index formula and the existence of maximal orders are right, and the M\_2(Q) case is proved; the claim for ramified B, the LMFDB's case, is open. Correction to the author's blocker, which said none of the local theory is on Tau Ceti main: its local-global half is there (above). The text error is new to the LMFDB issues: round T8's review noted it in review/T8\_mix.md and the 11-06 sweep used it as a reading, but neither lmfdb-knowl-edits\*.md nor the tracker's issues record it; the old text occurs once in the live text.
