ever a unique maximal order in a rational quaternion algebra': QuaternionAlgebra.not\_existsUnique\_isMaximalQuaternionOrder (not exists-unique maximal order, for every quaternion algebra over Q) and the stronger QuaternionAlgebra.exists\_isMaximalQuaternionOrder\_ne (every quaternion algebra over Q has two different maximal orders), so both readings of 'never unique' are proved. Proof (MaximalOrderConjugate.lean, 224 lines after the review): an isomorphism of Q-algebras maps orders to orders and maximal orders to maximal orders (IsQuaternionOrder.map, IsMaximalQuaternionOrder.map), so conjugates of maximal orders are maximal; no order is normalised by every unit (Subalgebra.exists\_map\_conj\_ne): for gamma in the normaliser of O, conjugation maps O onto itself, so trd(gamma)\^2/nrd(gamma), its trace on B, is an integer (the committed exists\_sq\_reducedTrace\_div\_eq\_intCast), while in B = (a, b / Q) (the committed exists\_nonempty\_algEquiv\_of\_finrank\_eq\_four) the unit gamma = 1 + s i, s = 3 den(a), has trd(gamma)\^2/nrd(gamma) = 4/(1 - a s\^2) with a s\^2 = 9 num(a) den(a) a nonzero integer, so 4 = z (1 - 9w), w != 0, is impossible (exists\_units\_forall\_sq\_reducedTrace\_div\_ne); hence a maximal order has a different conjugate, again maximal (IsMaximalQuaternionOrder.exists\_ne). Maximal orders exist: every quaternion algebra over Q has an order (QuaternionAlgebra.exists\_isQuaternionOrder: in (a, b / Q) the Z-span of 1, d1 i, d2 j, d1 d2 ij, d1, d2 the denominators of a, b, the range of the liftHom of a quaternion basis with integral structure constants, QuaternionAlgebra.Basis.isQuaternionOrder\_range\_liftHom), and every order lies in a maximal order (committed exists\_isMaximalQuaternionOrder\_le). This replaces round AJ's suggested route (conjugation by 1 + y/n and a limit) by a direct trace computation. (c) 'in many cases, there is exactly one B\^x-conjugacy class' (a stated fact, ruling of 2026-11-13: proved by an example): M2(Q), a quaternion algebra over Q (indefinite, discriminant 1), has exactly one class (MatrixMaximalOrder.lean, 164 lines after the review): M2(Z) (the range of the entrywise map) is a maximal order (isMaximalQuaternionOrder\_range\_mapMatrix: for x in an order containing M2(Z), x\_ij = tr(E\_ji x) = trd(E\_ji x) is an integer, reducedTrace\_matrix: trd = tr on M2(Q)); every order O of M2(Q) lies in a conjugate of M2(Z) (exists\_le\_map\_conj\_range\_mapMatrix: the Z-span L of the columns of the elements of O is a lattice of Q\^2 stable under O, and for gamma with columns a Z-basis of L, gamma\^-1 O gamma is integral, via Module.Basis.extendOfIsLattice\_repr); so the maximal orders of M2(Q) are exactly the conjugates gamma M2(Z) gamma\^-1 (isMaximalQuaternionOrder\_iff\_exists\_eq\_map\_conj), and any two are conjugate (exists\_eq\_map\_conj\_of\_isMaximalQuaternionOrder). Caveat on (c), kept from round AJ's review: M2(Q) is a rational quaternion algebra, as the sentence says, but the LMFDB uses this knowl for its Shimura curves, whose algebras are indefinite division algebras (disc(B) \> 1); there the fact is Eichler's theorem (every indefinite quaternion algebra over Q has type number 1, by strong approximation), in no tree. On 2026-11-13 the user asked for nf.sextic\_twin's remark to be proved by an example in the LMFDB's setting (a sextic number field) although the knowl's text allowed any field; the lead applied that ruling here on 2026-11-17 (review AM2 had put it as a question), so (c) stays a blocker. It does not change the outcome, which (b) keeps partial. (b) 'the number of B\^x-conjugacy classes is finite': not proved; for M2(Q) it follows from (c) (one class). Searched: no tree has the class set or the type number of an order; Tau Ceti's main (checkout-62bbc05) has IntegralOrder.IsOrder, IsMaximalOrder, IsOrder.exists\_le\_isMaximalOrder, Submodule.leftOrder\_isOrder (the left order of a lattice is an order) and the finite ideles of an order with their lattices (IntegralOrder